WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.833
(groovy music)

00:00:00.833 --> 00:00:02.640
- [Narrator] A drug used
in medication abortions,

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:04.680
the most common method to end a pregnancy,

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:06.780
is at the center of
the latest legal battle

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:08.730
over abortion access.

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:11.010
Federal judges in Texas and Washington

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:12.360
issued decisions that appear

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:14.280
directly at odds with one another.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:17.070
One suspends approval of
the drug, Mifepristone,

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:18.780
by the Food and Drug Administration.

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:20.520
The other prevents the FDA

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:23.670
from restricting access to
the drug in some states.

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:26.460
Laura Kusisto is a national
legal affairs reporter

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:28.500
and leads abortion
coverage for the journal.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:31.322
She explains what the decisions
mean and what comes next.

00:00:31.322 --> 00:00:33.257
(groovy music)

00:00:33.257 --> 00:00:34.350
(cheerful music)

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:36.060
- A Texas judge said that the FDA

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:40.050
had improperly approved a
drug known as Mifepristone.

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:42.120
He suspended that approval saying

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:43.830
that the FDA hadn't adequately considered

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:44.910
the safety of the drug.

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:47.460
They'd used the wrong procedure
for approving the drug.

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:49.140
The FDA had approved the drug

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:51.390
under a pathway that's
used to approve drugs

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:53.010
to treat life-threatening illnesses.

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:53.947
And the judge said,

00:00:53.947 --> 00:00:55.590
"Most of us would not understand pregnancy

00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:57.480
to be a serious life-threatening illness."

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:00.060
He also said that the
FDA had not adequately

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:02.040
taken into account possible concerns

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:03.776
about hemorrhage, about infection,

00:01:03.776 --> 00:01:06.360
about mental trauma
that women could suffer.

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:08.490
So this ruling is really significant

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:12.030
because it affects abortion
access not just in Texas,

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:13.110
but around the country.

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:15.240
If the judge's ruling does go into effect,

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:16.200
that would mean that the pill

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:17.310
could be pulled from the market.

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:18.600
And so, this is really kind of one

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:20.280
of the first developments we've seen

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:22.350
that has potential nationwide significance

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:24.030
since Roe v. Wade was overturned

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:27.570
- It's completely outta
bound, what the judge did.

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:30.720
- Journalists and lawyers
were all still digesting this

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:32.970
really kind of blockbuster
ruling out of Texas,

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:36.060
and we got this other really
quite unexpected ruling

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:37.770
from a judge in Washington State.

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:39.810
It appears that the judge there is saying

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:43.320
that the FDA can't take
additional action at the moment

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:46.830
to further constrain access
to the abortion pill.

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:48.930
Now, that ruling only applies in states

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:50.280
that were parties in the lawsuit,

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:52.860
but in those states,
there's like a real tension

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:54.810
between the rulings of these two judges.

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:57.600
There's a concern that this
could really destabilize

00:01:57.600 --> 00:01:59.700
the drug approval process in general,

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:02.760
if you have judges potentially
second guessing the FDA.

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:05.940
What I will say as a caveat
to that is that, you know,

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:07.050
this is abortion.

00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:11.190
This is not a typical drug,
an uncontroversial drug.

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:13.500
This is something that
has been at the center

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:16.920
of a political firestorm in
this country for five decades.

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:20.070
It's not a perfect
analogy for other drugs.

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:21.930
- It is contrary to what makes

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:24.240
for good public health policy

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:27.060
to allow courts and politicians

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:30.108
to tell the FDA what it should do.

00:02:30.108 --> 00:02:31.110
(groovy music)

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:32.130
- The Biden Administration

00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:33.960
moved quickly to appeal the ruling.

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:35.700
They filed something
with the Fifth Circuit,

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:39.600
the Court of Appeals, asking
for them to stay the ruling.

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:42.480
And they've asked for them to
make a decision by Thursday.

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:43.710
That could go one of two ways.

00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:46.470
They can give the Biden
administration what they want,

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:49.860
they can put this ruling
on hold or they can not.

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:51.510
They can allow it to go into effect.

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:53.040
And then in that instance,

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:54.960
I think you're very likely going to see

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:57.000
the Biden Administration
turnaround very quickly

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:59.340
and file an appeal with the Supreme Court.

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:02.430
- We stand by the FDA's
approval of Mifepristone

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:04.837
and we are prepared
for a long legal fight.

00:03:04.837 --> 00:03:07.420
(groovy music)

